Implement a new integer-to-sound formatter in `solution.rs`.

Public API:
```rust
pub trait AppendOnDivisor {
    fn append_on_divisor(&self, divisor: i64, word: &str, output: &mut String);
}
impl AppendOnDivisor for i64 { /* ... */ }
pub fn bell_code(value: i64) -> String;
```

`append_on_divisor` appends `word` exactly once when the receiver is evenly divisible by `divisor`; it appends nothing otherwise. If `divisor` is zero it must append nothing and must not panic. `bell_code` examines divisors in this exact order: 4 appends `Ding`, 6 appends `Dang`, and 9 appends `Dong`. If none apply, return the ordinary decimal representation of the input. Zero is divisible by every nonzero divisor, so it produces all three words. Negative values use normal Rust divisibility and fall back to their signed decimal form. Handle `i64::MIN` and `i64::MAX` without overflow. Do not include tests or `main`.

